value of $k$." 13194139534160 false] [17592186060377 1100 "The entry ${a}_{12}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ k & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}]$\nequals $18.$ Find the value of $k.$" 13194139534160 true] [17592186060377 1145 "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ k & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}| & =1|\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{aligned}|-2|\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ k & 0\\end{aligned}|+3|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ k & 6\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(0-24)-2(0-4k)+3(0-k) \\\\  & =8k-3k-24 \\\\  & =5k-24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{12}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{21}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{21} & =(-1)^{2+1}|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(0-18) \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{12}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{21} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5k-24}\\cdot 18 \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{5k-24}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that ${a}_{13}^{′}=18$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{18}{5k-24} & =18 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5k-24} & =1 \\\\ 1 & =5k-24 \\\\ 25 & =5k \\\\ k & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534160 true] [17592186279514 1017 #edb/ref 17592186279515 13194139534160 true] [17592186279514 1089 "q-18281" 13194139534160 true] [17592186279514 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534160 true] [17592186279514 1100 "Determine the entry ${a}_{21}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139534160 true] [17592186279514 1113 #uuid "5434732d-193f-528d-b9d5-29d58f90d985" 13194139534160 true] [17592186279514 1145 "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}